  Summary: shop_api was OOM-killed (exit 137) and restarted after its RSS climbed from about 87 MB to about 510 MB in roughly 40 seconds, while request rate rose from about 17 to 157 rps, almost all of it on GET /recs/{customer_id}. The recs handler writes every request into a module-level _CACHE under a key that includes time_ns, so entries are never reused or evicted and each one stores pre-rendered JSON for 10 clients. Customers on the storefront see restarts and dropped requests until the process is contained. I did not verify the enable_recs_cache flag state, so the flag's role is inferred from the code.
   - The shop_api container was OOM-killed with exit code 137 and restarted about 0.4 minutes before the alert.  [s1]
   - shop_api RSS rose from about 87 MB to about 510 MB between 08:20 and 08:21, against a limit of about 537 MB.  [s3, s1]
   - HTTP request rate rose from about 17 rps to about 157 rps starting at 08:20:29, just before the memory spike.  [s5]
   - The logs in the surge window are dominated by GET /recs/{customer_id} requests, which the static service map does not list.  [s7]
   - recommendations() inserts a new _CACHE entry on every call with key customer_id:time_ns(), and the cache has no eviction or size bound. Each entry stores rendered JSON for all 10 clients.  [s12]
   - The NoResultFound errors in render_receipt are a separate, lower-volume issue and do not explain the memory growth.  [s4, s8]
  Fix: Bound or remove the recs cache. Key it by customer_id (not time_ns) with a TTL and a max size, for example an LRU or TTLCache, and store the ids once rather than pre-rendering all 10 client variants. As an immediate mitigation, set the enable_recs_cache flag to off (a human should approve this) and restart shop_api. Then confirm RSS stays flat under the 157 rps load. Verify the flag state and review the recs traffic source.

